The way a glassblower shapes molten glass is fascinating. You start with a blob of incredibly hot glass, right? And to manipulate it, you're constantly managing the temperature - cooling it slightly to work with it, but keeping it hot enough to remain pliable. Sometimes, you'll use a reheating furnace to bring a section back up to temperature. The furnace doesn’t just blast the glass with heat; it radiates it, evenly coating the surface and ensuring uniform heating. The frequencies of the radiation matter; certain wavelengths are absorbed more readily than others, affecting how quickly the glass warms. 

