The intricacies of Roman road construction always fascinated me. They weren’t just laying down flat stones. They started with a deep foundation, a *statumen* layer of large rocks, then a *nucleus* of rubble and concrete, followed by a *pavimentum* of precisely fitted paving stones. Subsequent layers built upon that initial structure, each reflecting the engineering knowledge of the period and adapting to the specific terrain. You could trace the evolution of Roman road building by comparing early, rudimentary roads from the Republic with the sophisticated, multi-layered roads of the Empire—seeing the incremental improvements and adaptations in technique and materials over time. It's a tangible record of a changing understanding, a 'fossil' of technological progress, if you will.

That line of thought quite neatly applies to the question at hand. When we’re interested in understanding evolution, we’re looking for changes across time. Options A and D offer comparisons of individuals within a single generation, or snapshots of a single fish’s lifecycle. While interesting biologically, they don't represent evolutionary change *across* generations. A healed cut (B) is simply a response to injury; it says nothing about genetic shifts over generations. What we really need is a comparison across different time periods. A fish from long ago, representative of an earlier form, compared to a more recent fish reflecting a later form, would provide the best insight into how the species has changed over generations. That difference in time is the key to revealing evolutionary pathways.

